# OpenCode compatibility

## Supported combination

This package targets **OpenCode 2.0.29**. Its server/TUI definitions and typed adapter use the v2 API; v1 hosts are no longer supported. Version 0.2.0 replaces the earlier 1.19.0 integration.

| Component | Exact version |
| --- | --- |
| `@opencode/cli` (development/test CLI) | 2.0.29 |
| `@opencode/plugin`, `@opencode/client`, `@opencode/schema` | 2.0.29 |
| Bun | 1.4.3 |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 |

The real-server smoke probe builds, packs, installs and loads the package in an isolated Git repository. On macOS arm64 it exercises completed sessions, direct commands without model calls, event subscription, denied shell execution, linked-worktree observation restrictions, persistence, restart, explicit resume, interruption and status RPC. The deterministic local model proves the host boundary, not credentialed external-provider behavior. Native Linux/Windows results remain pending CI.

## Installation and entry points

Follow [README](../README.md). Configure `plugins` with the installed **package directory**, preferably a canonical, encoded file URL. Do not use the old `plugin` entry pointing to `dist/plugin.js`. OpenCode resolves `server.js` and `tui.js` from that directory; both have default definitions with `id: 'ralph'` and `setup(context)`. Package imports also expose `/server` and `/tui`.

Tests use `node_modules/@opencode/cli/bin/opencode.exe`, including on macOS/Linux. The manifest trusts this pinned CLI's postinstall script. Version changes require new runtime evidence, not just a dependency bump.

V2 activates external plugins asynchronously. Wait for command registration before dispatching external jobs; otherwise initial recovery may encounter newly recorded job intent. Local configuration changes require host activation/reload to complete.

Command registration can be visible before `plugin.list` publishes the active plugin. The installed-package probe also waits for that inventory entry before checking server/TUI features. This preserves the supported v2 mapping described in the [official plugin documentation](https://opencode.ai/v2/docs/build/plugins/); the ticket's proposed `@opencode-ai/plugin` import belongs to v1.

## Session adapter

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` accepts a narrow `AdapterHost`; `createAdapterClient` supplies it over the real v2 client and Ralph RPC. The plugin supplies the host's native session domain. V2 calls use flattened options and throw typed/transport failures rather than returning v1 `{ data, error, response }` envelopes.

```ts
const client = createAdapterClient(url, undefined, {
  Authorization: `Basic ${btoa('opencode:' + password)}`,
});
const session = await client.session.create({
  location: { directory }, title,
  model: { providerID, id: modelID },
});
const receipt = await client.session.prompt({ sessionID: session.id, text });
await client.session.wait({ sessionID: session.id });
const history = await client.session.context({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.interrupt({ sessionID: session.id });
await client.session.command({ sessionID: session.id, name: 'ralph-status', text: runId });
const commands = await client.command.list({ location: { directory } });
for await (const event of client.event.subscribe({ signal })) { /* v2 event.data */ }
```

Job intent is durable before session creation; the returned session ID is durable before prompting. Workspace selection occurs at session creation; subsequent calls target that session ID. Model fields map from Ralph's `{ providerID, modelID }` contract to v2 `{ providerID, id }`.

Prompt acknowledgment is not completion. The adapter waits, finds the delivered user message with the receipt's ID, and checks the subsequent turn. Success requires completed assistant text, `finish: 'stop'`, a successful idle record and no failed tools or session errors. Unexpected subsequent input makes correlation uncertain and returns missing output. Earlier failed/denied tools, including caught code-mode failures in tool metadata, cannot be erased by later assistant success.

Cancellation is recorded before interruption. Journals retain the existing `session.abort` operation kind for compatibility with persisted state; v2 uses `session.interrupt` underneath. A late-created cancelled session is interrupted without prompting. Stale results cannot update superseded attempts.

## Commands, observations and notifications

Commands register with `context.command.transform`; tools register with `context.tool.transform`. Status/pause/resume/stop execute directly. The exact command output is queued as a synthetic inbox item with `resume: false`, and a status event notifies the TUI without waking the model. Pending output can be inspected with `client.session.inbox.list`; it is consumed on the next session execution. Status never depends on generated assistant text.

V2 does not expose the plugin tool catalog as a public client endpoint. `src/opencode/bridge.ts` defines location-scoped Ralph RPC for tool discovery, observation preparation and status notification. Review sessions expose only `ralph_review_*` aliases of permitted observation tools. Aliases retain each original tool's permission action and execution function. Context filtering hides mutation tools and code-mode `execute`; the execution hook also rejects unapproved calls. Normal sessions retain their original tools and hide the observation aliases. Host denials are never changed to allows and no permissions are automatically approved.

The TUI entry listens for `rpc.ralph.status` in its location and shows an informational toast; cleanup unsubscribes. Server-only consumers can subscribe to status RPC events and use the durable status/dashboard. Interactive TUI rendering has unit contract coverage; native interactive/platform validation remains pending.

## Recovery and remaining limits

The canonical Git common directory owns `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`. Its fsynced JSONL journal remains authoritative; snapshots use atomic rename and writers use ownership-verified locks. Linked worktrees share state. Multiple v2 locations in one server recover that state once and only the last location's cleanup stops shared work. Restart still requires explicit resume and never resets attempts or review budgets. No new state schema or migration is needed for existing journals.

V2 exposes session forms, but Ralph's human-input/approval provenance workflow is still absent. `requestUserInput` remains explicitly unsupported; neither assistant text nor API-created user messages establish human approval. The command setup workflow now validates correlated host-supplied approvals and lifecycle execution in fixtures, with production execution permissions delegated to `ctx.permission.assert`. Production command approval, interview/PRD approval, baseline preparation and issue scheduling/execution remain unfinished. See [README](../README.md) and [open questions](wiki/open-questions.md).

## Reproduce

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun run test
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
bun run test:package
```

On PowerShell set `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'` before running the smoke suite. The local probe uses an isolated test-only Basic-auth password. The separate live probe requires `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model` and the server's `OPENCODE_SMOKE_PASSWORD`; without its opt-in/endpoint/model it is skipped. Do not put credentials in package resources or fixture data.

## Issue 1 verification — 2026-10-14

- macOS arm64, Bun 1.4.3: typecheck and lint passed; all 19 issue-1 integration tests passed.
- `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun test tests/smoke/opencode.test.ts`: **2 passed, 1 skipped**. The fresh-source install uses only the manifest/lockfile and an empty package cache. The installed-package probe builds, packs and loads Ralph in real OpenCode 2.0.29, correlates its local model response through the public router/adapter, preserves permission denial, observes events and verifies status after host restart.
- Red/green evidence: the empty-cache install failed with `ENOENT extracting tarball from opencode-ralph-plugin` before removing the accidental self-dependency from the manifest and regenerating the lockfile. It then passed. A warm cache had concealed the missing archive. Package installation must not depend on a previously built copy of itself.
- The real probe initially failed on an unpublished plugin inventory entry; the probe now waits for active inventory independently of command registration, retaining its TUI assertion.
- Fresh-install checks are gated because they download dependencies; the deterministic issue-1 suite requires neither network model access nor paid calls. Credentialed external-provider execution was skipped. Linux and Windows have CI jobs but were not run here; interactive TUI and human-response provenance remain unverified/unsupported as described above.
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";
import {
  mkdir,
  readFile,
  realpath,
  rename,
  rm,
  writeFile,
} from "node:fs/promises";
import { join } from "node:path";
import {
  type ApprovedCommandConfig,
  commandConfigHash,
  parseCommandConfig,
} from "../config/commands.js";
import type { OpenCodeAdapter } from "../opencode/adapter.js";
import {
  createApprovedEnvironment,
  disposeEnvironment,
  prepareEnvironment,
} from "../process/environment.js";
import type { CommandResult } from "../process/runner.js";
import type { RunStore } from "../state/run-store.js";
import { runtimeSecretValues } from "../state/sanitize.js";

export interface ProjectSetupOptions {
  workspace: string;
  sessionId: string;
}
export interface ProjectSetupStatus {
  status: "rejected" | "not-ready" | "verified";
  hash?: string;
  revision?: number;
  platform?: string;
  reason?: string;
  results?: CommandResult[];
  cleanupError?: string;
}
async function performSetup(
  options: ProjectSetupOptions & { store: RunStore; adapter: OpenCodeAdapter },
): Promise<ProjectSetupStatus> {
  const { store, adapter, workspace, sessionId } = options;
  const runId = `setup-${randomUUID()}`;
  await store.createRun(runId);
  const previous = await readFile(
    join(workspace, ".ralph/commands.json"),
    "utf8",
  ).catch((e) => {
    if (e.code === "ENOENT") return undefined;
    throw e;
  });
  const revision = previous
    ? parseCommandConfig(JSON.parse(previous)).revision + 1
    : 1;
  const instructions = await readFile(
    new URL("../../skills/command-discovery/SKILL.md", import.meta.url),
    "utf8",
  );
  const job = {
    runId,
    jobId: "command-discovery",
    attempt: 1,
    role: "command-discovery",
    workspace: await realpath(workspace),
    inputRevisions: { commands: revision },
    baseCommit: "",
    readOnly: true,
    prompt: `${instructions}\nExplore this repository using read-only tools. Return ONLY a complete JSON command configuration: schemaVersion:1, revision:${revision}, rationale, commands with id, phase(setup/check/start/readiness/cleanup), executable, args array, cwd relative to workspace, timeoutMs, platforms(darwin/linux/win32), env mapping target variable names to source variable names; optional explicit shell. Explain absent scripts honestly in rationale, unsupported platforms in unsupportedPlatforms. Require a check per supported platform; order lifecycle phases. Never execute commands, write files, or include secret values. This proposal is not approval.`,
  };
  const result = await adapter.dispatchJob(job);
  if (result.outcome !== "completed" || !result.text)
    return {
      status: "not-ready",
      reason: result.error?.message ?? "No discovery output",
    };
  let config: ApprovedCommandConfig;
  try {
    config = parseCommandConfig(JSON.parse(result.text));
    if (config.revision !== revision)
      throw new Error("Unexpected command revision");
  } catch (e) {
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: String(e) };
  }
  const references = config.commands
    .flatMap((c) => Object.values(c.env))
    .map((name) => process.env[name] ?? "")
    .filter(Boolean);
  if (
    [...runtimeSecretValues(), ...references].some((secret) =>
      JSON.stringify(config).includes(JSON.stringify(secret).slice(1, -1)),
    )
  )
    return {
      status: "not-ready",
      reason:
        "Proposal contains a runtime secret value; use environment references",
    };
  const hash = commandConfigHash(config);
  const request = {
    runId,
    sessionId,
    workspace,
    requestId: randomUUID(),
    revision,
    question: `Approve this exact command configuration (hash ${hash}) by replying approve ${hash}, or reject:\n${JSON.stringify(config, null, 2)}`,
  };
  await adapter.publishStatus(sessionId, workspace, request.question);
  const approval = await adapter.requestUserInput(request);
  if (!approval.ok)
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: approval.error.message };
  if (
    approval.requestId !== request.requestId ||
    approval.revision !== revision ||
    approval.sessionId !== sessionId ||
    approval.source !== "user" ||
    !approval.messageId ||
    approval.text.trim() !== `approve ${hash}`
  )
    return {
      status: "rejected",
      reason: "Rejected, modified or stale approval",
    };
  const current = await readFile(
    join(workspace, ".ralph/commands.json"),
    "utf8",
  ).catch((e) => {
    if (e.code === "ENOENT") return undefined;
    throw e;
  });
  if (current !== previous)
    return {
      status: "rejected",
      reason: "Configuration changed while awaiting approval",
    };
  const executionJob = {
    ...job,
    jobId: "command-verification",
    sessionId,
    readOnly: false,
  };
  if (!(await store.recordIntent(executionJob)))
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: "Stale verification intent" };
  const saveOperation = {
    operationId: `commands-${randomUUID()}`,
    kind: "project.commands" as const,
    job: executionJob,
    status: "intent" as const,
    ref: JSON.stringify({
      hash,
      revision,
      requestId: request.requestId,
      sessionId,
      messageId: approval.messageId,
    }),
  };
  if (!(await store.recordOperation(saveOperation)))
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: "Stale configuration save intent" };
  await mkdir(join(workspace, ".ralph"), { recursive: true });
  if (
    (await realpath(join(workspace, ".ralph"))) !==
    join(await realpath(workspace), ".ralph")
  )
    return {
      status: "not-ready",
      reason:
        "Configuration directory must be inside the workspace without symlinks",
    };
  // Keep each approved revision outside the working tree for future run pinning.
  const revisions = join(store.root, "command-revisions");
  await mkdir(revisions, { recursive: true });
  const archive = join(revisions, `${hash}.json`);
  const archived = await readFile(archive, "utf8").catch((error) => {
    if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return undefined;
    throw error;
  });
  if (archived !== undefined) {
    if (commandConfigHash(parseCommandConfig(JSON.parse(archived))) !== hash)
      throw new Error("Immutable command archive is corrupt");
  } else await atomicJson(archive, config);
  const temporary = join(workspace, `.ralph/commands-${randomUUID()}.tmp`);
  try {
    await writeFile(temporary, `${JSON.stringify(config, null, 2)}\n`, {
      flag: "wx",
    });
    await rename(temporary, join(workspace, ".ralph/commands.json"));
  } finally {
    await rm(temporary, { force: true });
  }
  if (!(await store.recordOperation({ ...saveOperation, status: "completed" })))
    return {
      status: "not-ready",
      reason: "Stale configuration save completion",
    };
  const environment = createApprovedEnvironment({
    store,
    adapter,
    job: executionJob,
    config,
  });
  let reason: string | undefined;
  try {
    await prepareEnvironment(environment, executionJob);
  } catch (e) {
    reason = String(e);
  }
  const cleanup = await disposeEnvironment(environment, executionJob).catch(
    (e) => ({ status: "failed", reason: String(e) }),
  );
  const status: ProjectSetupStatus = {
    status: reason || cleanup.status !== "completed" ? "not-ready" : "verified",
    revision,
    hash,
    platform: process.platform,
    results: environment.results,
    ...(reason ? { reason } : {}),
    ...(cleanup.status !== "completed" ? { cleanupError: cleanup.reason } : {}),
  };
  const recorded = await store.recordResult({
    ...executionJob,
    outcome: status.status === "verified" ? "completed" : "failed",
    evidence: environment.results.map((r) => r.commandId),
    text: JSON.stringify(status),
  });
  if (!recorded)
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: "Stale verification completion" };
  await atomicJson(join(workspace, ".ralph/verification.json"), status);
  await adapter.publishStatus(
    sessionId,
    workspace,
    JSON.stringify(status, null, 2),
  );
  return status;
}

async function atomicJson(path: string, value: unknown) {
  const temporary = `${path}-${randomUUID()}.tmp`;
  try {
    await writeFile(temporary, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\n`, {
      flag: "wx",
    });
    await rename(temporary, path);
  } finally {
    await rm(temporary, { force: true });
  }
}
export async function projectStatus(
  workspace: string,
): Promise<ProjectSetupStatus> {
  try {
    const config = parseCommandConfig(
      JSON.parse(
        await readFile(join(workspace, ".ralph/commands.json"), "utf8"),
      ),
    );
    const status = JSON.parse(
      await readFile(join(workspace, ".ralph/verification.json"), "utf8"),
    ) as ProjectSetupStatus;
    if (
      status.hash !== commandConfigHash(config) ||
      status.platform !== process.platform ||
      status.revision !== config.revision
    )
      return {
        status: "not-ready",
        reason: "Configuration or host differs from verification",
      };
    return status;
  } catch {
    return { status: "not-ready", reason: "No valid command verification" };
  }
}
export async function assertProjectReady(workspace: string) {
  const status = await projectStatus(workspace);
  if (status.status !== "verified")
    throw new Error(
      status.reason ??
        status.cleanupError ??
        "Project commands are not verified",
    );
}

/** Canonical repository lock covers discovery, approval, save and verification. */
export async function setupProject(
  options: ProjectSetupOptions & { store: RunStore; adapter: OpenCodeAdapter },
): Promise<ProjectSetupStatus> {
  const lock = join(options.store.root, "project-setup.lock");
  try {
    await mkdir(lock);
  } catch (e) {
    if ((e as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "EEXIST")
      return {
        status: "not-ready",
        reason:
          "Project setup is already active; interrupted locks require reconciliation",
      };
    throw e;
  }
  try {
    const result = await performSetup(options);
    await options.adapter.publishStatus(
      options.sessionId,
      options.workspace,
      JSON.stringify(result, null, 2),
    );
    return result;
  } finally {
    await rm(lock, { recursive: true });
  }
}
